          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have been diagnosed with high blood pressure.",
          "answer": "High blood pressure, also known as hypertension, is when the pressure in your arteries stays high over a period of time, not just from a single measurement. Blood pressure is written as two numbers: the systolic pressure (when the heart contracts) and the diastolic pressure (between beats). Both are measured in millimeters of mercury (mm Hg). Blood pressure naturally changes throughout the day due to activity, stress, and other factors, which is why a diagnosis is based on several measurements, not just one. High blood pressure often has no symptoms and is usually found during routine health checks. (condition-hypertension)\n\nHow high blood pressure is classified can vary depending on the guidelines used:\n\n*   **United States guidance** often uses 130/80 mm Hg as the threshold for diagnosing hypertension. (guideline-hypertension-diagnosis-and-bp-targets, guideline-coronary-artery-disease-secondary-prevention)\n*   **European guidance** considers office hypertension to start at 140/90 mm Hg. A blood pressure reading between 130-139/80-89 mm Hg is often described as \"high-normal blood pressure\" rather than stage 1 hypertension. (guideline-hypertension-diagnosis-and-bp-targets)\n\nWhen the systolic and diastolic numbers fall into different categories, the higher category generally applies. The term \"elevated\" blood pressure describes a group with a higher future risk, where lifestyle changes are often recommended. (guideline-hypertension-diagnosis-and-bp-targets)\n\nFor medical coding, simple hypertension without organ involvement is classified under I10 in the ICD-10 system. If high blood pressure affects organs like the heart or kidneys, or has an identifiable underlying cause, different specific codes are used. For example, hypertensive heart disease or chronic kidney disease with hypertension would use combination codes. (coding-hypertension-i10)\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
